======================================================================
CLAIM (1) PRECISE: at GRID points a/14, is a 7-runner ever the min?
======================================================================
  Odd-grid violations (7-runner strict min): 0
  => At odd grid points, 7-runner is always at 1/2, never the binding min.
  => CLAIM (1) HOLDS: a 7-runner is FREE at odd grid (its own antipode);
     at even grid it sits in section 0 (dist 0) = harmless, only removes
     even grid points as loneliness witnesses. It never makes a config harder
     at the grid; off-grid is a separate matter (it can lower M off-grid).

======================================================================
CLAIM (2): can a transform move a section-0 (==0 mod14) runner to section-7?
======================================================================
  (Z/14)* units: [1, 3, 5, 9, 11, 13]
  u*0 mod14 for each unit: [0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0] -> all 0. Units FIX section 0.
  Reversal a->-a == mult by 13(=-1): -0==0, -7==7 mod14. FIXES both 0 and 7.
  => Section 0 is a FIXED POINT of every (Z/14)* unit (0*u=0). So NO unit
     can move a section-0 runner to section-7. CONFIRMED structurally.

  Mult by 2: 0->0 (still section 0). 7->14==0 mod14! So *2 sends a 7-runner
    INTO section 0 (free->parked, WRONG direction). And 2 is not invertible mod14.
  Additive shift v->v+7: sends 0->7 (parked->free!) and 7->14==0 (free->parked).
    BUT v+7 changes the actual runner SPEED -> a DIFFERENT runner, different config.
    Does v->v+7 preserve M? Test:
    [14, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5]: M=1/6  ->  shift+7 [21, 8, 9, 10, 11, 12]: M=8/29  (preserved: False)
    [28, 3, 5, 9, 11]: M=14/39  ->  shift+7 [35, 10, 12, 16, 18]: M=1/3  (preserved: False)
    [14, 2, 4, 8, 16]: M=1/3  ->  shift+7 [21, 9, 11, 15, 23]: M=1/2  (preserved: False)

======================================================================
CLAIM (2) EXPLANATION: why 0 and 7 are BOTH fixed but distinguishable
======================================================================
  r=0: CRT (mod2,mod7)=(0,0)
  r=7: CRT (mod2,mod7)=(1,0)
  Units fix the mod-2 coordinate -> cannot map parity-0 (sec 0) to parity-1 (sec 7).
  The ONLY maps mixing them are additive (shift by odd), which change speeds.

======================================================================
CLAIM (3): full-config reversal. Two reversals to distinguish:
======================================================================
Reversal B: S -> {(-v) mod 14 represented as 14-(v%14)} acting on RESIDUES.
  This is mult by -1 (a unit). Section 0 (0)->0, section 7 (7)->7: BOTH FIXED.
  Other sections swap: 1<->13, 2<->12, ... 6<->8. The 0/7 occupants are pinned.

  At grid a/14, section of v is v*a mod14. Replacing a -> -a == 14-a (also a grid
  point) sends section s -> -s mod14. So the grid picture is symmetric under a->-a,
  which fixes section 0 and section 7 and swaps s<->14-s. M unchanged (same grid set).
  Verify g(S,a/14)==g(S,(14-a)/14) for all tested a: True

